Text similarity: word trigrams:
```python
def text_key(rec):
    parts=[]
    for k,s in strings(rec):
        kl=k.lower()
        if any(w in kl for w in ("finding","hard_failure","statement","objection","reason","summary","positive","minimal_fix","score_basis","scope")):
            parts.append(s.lower())
    toks=re.findall(r"[a-z0-9_]+", " ".join(parts))
    toks=[t for t in toks if len(t)>=3]
    return set(zip(toks,toks[1:],toks[2:])) if len(toks)>=3 else set(toks)  # mix sizes...
```
Mixing tuple sizes in a set is fine (hashable), but Jaccard across different cardinalities is odd; for short texts use set(toks) (1-tuples) instead: I'll use `set(toks)` for short and trigrams for long, and in Jaccard just compare sets; if both empty → not similar unless exact text equal. Simpler and defensible: use word 2-grams if >=2 tokens else unigrams; both items use the same function → consistent.

